Page 3 ke LOOKUP & REFERENCE par click karte hi page 4 par dikhenge:

[1] XLOOKUP
- Explanation: XLOOKUP Excel ka sabse modern lookup tool hai jo VLOOKUP aur HLOOKUP dono ki kamiya door karta hai. Ye left-to-right aur right-to-left dono taraf data khoj sakta hai.
- Purpose: Kisi ID, Barcode, ya Item Name ke aadhar par uski details (Price, Stock, Category) fast dhoondna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Master Table (Cols A-C): [A: Item Code, B: Item Name, C: Price]
  Row 2: P101 | Denim Jeans | 1200
  Row 3: P102 | Cotton Shirt | 800
  Target: E2 me Item Code 'P102' likhne par F2 me Price chahiye.
  Formula in F2: =XLOOKUP(E2, A2:A3, C2:C3, "Not Found")
  Result: 800

[2] VLOOKUP
- Explanation: Vertical Lookup data ko top to bottom search karta hai aur specified column se value lata hai.
- Purpose: Master list se numeric column index ke aadhar par details match karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Table Range A2:C10. Search ID "P101" (Cell E2 me hai).
  Formula: =VLOOKUP(E2, A2:C10, 2, FALSE)
  Result: "Denim Jeans" (Column 2 se name uthaya).

[3] HLOOKUP
- Explanation: Horizontal Lookup data ko left to right rows me dhoondta hai aur specified row se result lata hai.
- Purpose: Quarter-wise ya Month-wise horizontal headers wale data se figures nikalna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Row 1 (Months): Jan | Feb | Mar
  Row 2 (Sales):  50000 | 65000 | 80000
  Formula: =HLOOKUP("Feb", A1:C2, 2, FALSE)
  Result: 65000

[4] INDEX & MATCH
- Explanation: INDEX specific row aur column position se value uthata hai, jabki MATCH batata hai ki koi value kis position par hai. Dono milkar VLOOKUP se zyada flexible lookup banate hain.
- Purpose: Dynamic multi-directional lookup aur heavy files me fast calculation.
- Scenario & Example:
  Names in A2:A5, Salaries in B2:B5. Target Name "Amit" in D2.
  Formula: =INDEX(B2:B5, MATCH(D2, A2:A5, 0))
  Result: Amit ki Salary.

[5] XMATCH
- Explanation: MATCH ka advanced version jo default exact match perform karta hai aur reverse search bhi kar sakta hai.
- Purpose: Array me kisi item ki exact relative position fast khojna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Items in A2:A5 ("Shirt", "Jeans", "Jacket"). Search "Jeans".
  Formula: =XMATCH("Jeans", A2:A5)
  Result: 2

[6] LOOKUP
- Explanation: Ye older compatibility function hai jo vector ya array me approximate/exact value dhoondta hai (data sorted hona chahiye).
- Purpose: Simple ranges me quick status/grade mapping.
- Scenario & Example:
  Marks Range A2:A5 (0, 35, 60, 80), Grades B2:B5 ("F", "C", "B", "A").
  Formula: =LOOKUP(75, A2:A5, B2:B5)
  Result: "B"

[7] INDIRECT
- Explanation: Ye text string ko valid cell reference me convert karta hai.
- Purpose: Dynamic sheet switching aur dependent drop-down lists banana.
- Scenario & Example:
  Cell A1 me text likha hai "B5". Cell B5 me value hai 5000.
  Formula: =INDIRECT(A1)
  Result: 5000

[8] OFFSET
- Explanation: Ye kisi starting cell se specified rows aur columns door ke cell ya range ka reference deta hai.
- Purpose: Dynamic ranges aur moving averages calculate karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Start Cell A1. 2 rows neeche aur 1 column right ka data chahiye.
  Formula: =OFFSET(A1, 2, 1)
  Result: Cell B3 ki value.

[9] FORMULATEXT
- Explanation: Ye kisi cell me lage hue formula ko text ke roop me screen par dikhata hai.
- Purpose: Audit, documentation aur training sheets me formula reveal karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Cell C2 me formula `=A2+B2` laga hai.
  Formula in D2: =FORMULATEXT(C2)
  Result: "=A2+B2"

[10] HYPERLINK
- Explanation: Ye cell me clickable shortcut link banata hai jo kisi file, web page ya sheet location par le jata hai.
- Purpose: Navigation dashboards aur document tracking systems.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =HYPERLINK("https://google.com", "Open Google")
  Result: "Open Google" text par click karte hi link open hoga.

[11] GETPIVOTDATA
- Explanation: Ye Pivot Table se specific aggregated data safely extract karta hai.
- Purpose: Pivot table refresh hone ke baad bhi exact metrics ko dashboard cards par lana.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =GETPIVOTDATA("Sales", $A$3, "Region", "North")
  Result: North Region ki Total Sales.

[12] ROW & ROWS
- Explanation: ROW cell ki row number batata hai. ROWS poori range me kul kitni rows hain wo ginta hai.
- Purpose: Dynamic serial numbers aur matrix size calculation.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula 1: =ROW(C5) -> Result: 5
  Formula 2: =ROWS(A1:A10) -> Result: 10

[13] COLUMN & COLUMNS
- Explanation: COLUMN cell ka column number batata hai. COLUMNS poori range ke total columns ginta hai.
- Purpose: Dynamic formula dragging aur matrix dimension checks.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula 1: =COLUMN(C1) -> Result: 3
  Formula 2: =COLUMNS(A1:D5) -> Result: 4



Page 3 ke MODERN DYNAMIC ARRAYS par click karte hi page 4 par dikhenge:

[1] FILTER
- Explanation: Specified condition ke aadhar par source data se matching rows ko automatically filter karke alag jagah display karta hai.
- Purpose: Live dynamic reports banana bina manual filter apply kiye.
- Scenario & Example:
  Data A2:B10 [A: City, B: Sales]. Filter only "Delhi" sales.
  Formula: =FILTER(A2:B10, A2:A10="Delhi", "No Data")
  Result: Delhi ki saari rows automatically spill ho jayengi.

[2] UNIQUE
- Explanation: Kisi list se duplicate values hata kar sirf unique items ki list nikalta hai.
- Purpose: Dropdown list ke liye master distinct values banana.
- Scenario & Example:
  List A2:A8 ("Delhi", "Mumbai", "Delhi", "Jaipur", "Mumbai").
  Formula: =UNIQUE(A2:A8)
  Result: Delhi, Mumbai, Jaipur

[3] SORT
- Explanation: Single column ya multi-column array ko ascending ya descending order me automatically arrange karta hai.
- Purpose: Dynamic Top-Sales ya Ranked reporting.
- Scenario & Example:
  Sales Data Range B2:B10 ko descending order me lagana.
  Formula: =SORT(B2:B10, 1, -1)
  Result: Highest se Lowest sales values arranged.

[4] SORTBY
- Explanation: Ek array ko kisi doosre array/column ke values ke aadhar par sort karta hai.
- Purpose: Employee names ko unke Salary ya Score ke basis par arrange karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Names A2:A5, Scores B2:B5. Names ko Score ke basis par sort karna.
  Formula: =SORTBY(A2:A5, B2:B5, -1)
  Result: Highest scorer ka naam sabse upar.

[5] SEQUENCE
- Explanation: Specified rows, columns, start, aur step values ke basis par numbers ki sequential series generate karta hai.
- Purpose: Automated 1 to 100 serial numbers ya date series create karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =SEQUENCE(5, 1, 100, 10)
  Result: 100, 110, 120, 130, 140 vertically display hoga.

[6] RANDARRAY
- Explanation: Specified rows aur columns me random decimal ya integer numbers fill karta hai.
- Purpose: Testing, simulation, aur sample datasets generate karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =RANDARRAY(3, 2, 1, 50, TRUE)
  Result: 3x2 grid me 1 se 50 ke beech ke whole numbers.

[7] CHOOSEROWS & CHOOSECOLS
- Explanation: CHOOSEROWS array me se specific rows extract karta hai, CHOOSECOLS specific columns uthata hai.
- Purpose: Large table me se kewal required columns/rows ki customized view banana.
- Scenario & Example:
  Table A2:D10 me se Column 1 (Name) aur Column 4 (Salary) uthana.
  Formula: =CHOOSECOLS(A2:D10, 1, 4)
  Result: Kewal Name aur Salary ka filtered table.

[8] TAKE & DROP
- Explanation: TAKE array ke start/end se specified rows/cols rakhne ke liye hai; DROP unhe remove/skip karne ke liye hai.
- Purpose: Top N records nikalna ya Header/Footer rows skip karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Top 3 Sales rows uthana: =TAKE(A2:B10, 3)
  First 2 rows chhod kar baaki data lena: =DROP(A2:B10, 2)

[9] EXPAND
- Explanation: Kisi chhotey array ko specified dimensions tak bada karta hai aur khali cells ko custom value se bharta hai.
- Purpose: Un-even datasets ko combine karne ke liye standard size me lana.
- Scenario & Example:
  Array A1:B2 (2x2) ko 4x2 banana aur new cells me "N/A" bharna.
  Formula: =EXPAND(A1:B2, 4, 2, "N/A")

[10] TOCOL & TOROW
- Explanation: TOCOL poore 2D matrix ko single vertical column me aur TOROW single horizontal row me convert karta hai.
- Purpose: Multi-column tags/addresses ko vertical list me consolidate karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  3x3 Grid (A1:C3) ko ek vertical list banana.
  Formula: =TOCOL(A1:C3)

[11] WRAPROWS & WRAPCOLS
- Explanation: Single row/column list ko specified length par break karke 2D grid me wrap karta hai.
- Purpose: Flat lists ko tabular grid layouts me formatting dena.
- Scenario & Example:
  10 items ki vertical list ko 2 columns ke grid me convert karna.
  Formula: =WRAPROWS(A1:A10, 2)



Page 3 ke LOGICAL & DECISION MAKING par click karte hi Page 4 par dikhenge:

[1] IF
- Explanation: Condition check karta hai; True hone par pehla result aur False hone par doosra result deta hai.
- Purpose: Basic decision making (Pass/Fail, Eligible/Not Eligible).
- Scenario & Example:
  Marks Cell A2 = 45.
  Formula: =IF(A2>=33, "Pass", "Fail") -> Result: "Pass"

[2] IFS
- Explanation: Multiple conditions ko bina nested IF ke sequentially check karta hai.
- Purpose: Multi-tier grading ya slab-wise commission rates.
- Scenario & Example:
  Score Cell A2 = 85.
  Formula: =IFS(A2>=90, "A+", A2>=80, "A", A2>=70, "B") -> Result: "A"

[3] AND, OR, XOR, NOT
- Explanation:
  - AND: Jab SAARI conditions true ho.
  - OR: Jab KOI BHI EK condition true ho.
  - XOR: Jab conditions me se ODD number of conditions true hon.
  - NOT: Result ko reverse kar deta hai (True ko False, False ko True).
- Purpose: Complex validation rules construct karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Age A2=25, Exp B2=3. Formula: =AND(A2>=18, B2>=2) -> Result: TRUE

[4] SWITCH
- Explanation: Kisi single expression ki value ko multiple cases se match karke corresponding result deta hai.
- Purpose: Code numbers ko text labels me mapping karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Day Code Cell A2 = 2.
  Formula: =SWITCH(A2, 1, "Mon", 2, "Tue", 3, "Wed", "Invalid") -> Result: "Tue"

[5] IFERROR & IFNA
- Explanation: Formula me error (#N/A, #VALUE!, #DIV/0!) aane par custom clean message display karta hai.
- Purpose: Professional clean reports banana bina ugly error tags ke.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =IFERROR(10/0, "Calculation Error") -> Result: "Calculation Error"

[6] ISNUMBER, ISTEXT, ISBLANK, ISERROR, ISEVEN, ISODD
- Explanation: Cell ki property check karke TRUE ya FALSE return karte hain.
- Purpose: Data auditing, input validation aur conditional formatting logic.
- Scenario & Example:
  Cell A2 = "Hello". Formula: =ISTEXT(A2) -> Result: TRUE



CATEGORY 1.4: ADVANCED LAMBDA & VARIABLES

[1] LET
- Explanation: Formula ke andar custom variables aur intermediate calculations ko naam assign karta hai.
- Purpose: Calculation speed badhana aur complex formulas ko readable banana.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =LET(Price, 100, Tax, 18, Price + (Price * Tax / 100))
  Result: 118

[2] LAMBDA
- Explanation: Bina VBA/Macros ke apna custom reusable Excel function banana.
- Purpose: Company-specific complex calculations ko simple one-line function banana.
- Scenario & Example:
  Custom GST Function: =LAMBDA(amount, amount * 0.18)
  Name Manager me save karke `=GST(500)` use kar sakte hain.

[3] MAP, REDUCE, SCAN
- Explanation:
  - MAP: Array ke har element par LAMBDA apply karke new array banata hai.
  - REDUCE: Array ko single value me accumulate/combine karta hai.
  - SCAN: Array par running calculation (e.g. running total) karta hai.
- Purpose: Complex looping logic formulas.
- Scenario & Example:
  Running total of 1, 2, 3: =SCAN(0, A1:A3, LAMBDA(a, b, a+b)) -> Result: 1, 3, 6

[4] MAKEARRAY, BYROW, BYCOL
- Explanation:
  - MAKEARRAY: Custom dimensions aur formula ke basis par synthetic array banata hai.
  - BYROW / BYCOL: Grid ke har Row ya Column par individully operation run karta hai.
- Purpose: Matrix generation aur row-level dynamic aggregations.
- Scenario & Example:
  Data A1:C3 ke har Row ka sum: =BYROW(A1:C3, LAMBDA(r, SUM(r)))



Page 3 ke TEXT & STRING MANIPULATION par click karte hi Page 4 par dikhenge:

[1] TEXTSPLIT, TEXTBEFORE, TEXTAFTER
- Explanation:
  - TEXTSPLIT: Delimiter ke basis par text ko multiple columns/rows me todta hai.
  - TEXTBEFORE: Delimiter se PEHLE ka text extract karta hai.
  - TEXTAFTER: Delimiter ke BAAD ka text extract karta hai.
- Purpose: Full Names se First/Last Name alag karna ya Email se Username/Domain extract karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Email in A2 = "rahul.sharma@gmail.com"
  Formula 1: =TEXTBEFORE(A2, "@") -> Result: "rahul.sharma"
  Formula 2: =TEXTAFTER(A2, "@") -> Result: "gmail.com"

[2] TEXTJOIN & CONCAT
- Explanation: TEXTJOIN custom delimiter aur ignore-blank option ke sath text merge karta hai. CONCAT simple list join karta hai.
- Purpose: Multiple columns ke address/names ko ek single cell me jodne ke liye.
- Scenario & Example:
  A2="Delhi", B2="India".
  Formula: =TEXTJOIN(", ", TRUE, A2, B2) -> Result: "Delhi, India"

[3] LEFT, RIGHT, MID, LEN
- Explanation: Text me se specified characters extract karna (LEFT=start se, RIGHT=end se, MID=beech se). LEN text ki kul length ginta hai.
- Purpose: Serial Codes, Account Numbers, ya State Codes separate karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Code A2 = "DL-110001".
  Formula 1: =LEFT(A2, 2) -> Result: "DL"
  Formula 2: =LEN(A2) -> Result: 9

[4] SEARCH & FIND
- Explanation: Text string me kisi character/word ki position batate hain. FIND case-sensitive hai, SEARCH case-insensitive.
- Purpose: Dynamic text trimming logic ke liye character position dhoondna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Text A2 = "Invoice # 450". Formula: =SEARCH("#", A2) -> Result: 9

[5] SUBSTITUTE & REPLACE
- Explanation: SUBSTITUTE specific word ko replace karta hai; REPLACE specific character position ke basis par replace karta hai.
- Purpose: Formatting errors sahi karna (jaise spaces/hyphens replace karna).
- Scenario & Example:
  Phone A2 = "9876-543-210".
  Formula: =SUBSTITUTE(A2, "-", "") -> Result: "9876543210"

[6] TRIM, CLEAN, PROPER, UPPER, LOWER
- Explanation:
  - TRIM: Extra spaces hatata hai.
  - CLEAN: Non-printable system characters hatata hai.
  - PROPER: First letter Capital karta hai.
  - UPPER / LOWER: All Caps ya All Small case me badalta hai.
- Purpose: Raw imported dirty data ko clean aur standardize karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Text A2 = "  rahul VERMA ".
  Formula: =PROPER(TRIM(A2)) -> Result: "Rahul Verma"

[7] TEXT, VALUE, REPT, CHAR, CODE
- Explanation:
  - TEXT: Numbers ko specific custom format me convert karta hai.
  - VALUE: Text-based numbers ko numeric format me badalta hai.
  - REPT: Text ko specified times repeat karta hai (in-cell bar charts ke liye).
- Scenario & Example:
  Date A2 = 08/09/2026. Formula: =TEXT(A2, "mmmm-yyyy") -> Result: "September-2026"
  Formula 2: =REPT("|", 5) -> Result: "|||||"



Page 3 ke MATH & CONDITIONAL AGGREGATIONS par click karte hi Page 4 par dikhenge:

[1] SUM, SUMIF, SUMIFS
- Explanation: SUM simple jodta hai. SUMIF 1 condition ke basis par jodta hai. SUMIFS Multiple conditions ke basis par jodta hai.
- Purpose: Region-wise, Date-wise, ya Category-wise total sales nikalna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Region (Col A), Sales (Col B).
  Formula: =SUMIFS(B2:B10, A2:A10, "North") -> Result: North Region ka total sales.

[2] COUNT, COUNTA, COUNTBLANK, COUNTIF, COUNTIFS
- Explanation: Numbers, Non-blanks, Blanks, 1-Condition count, aur Multi-Condition counts karne ke functions.
- Purpose: Attendance tracking, item frequency, aur filtered counts.
- Scenario & Example:
  Status (Col A), Dept (Col B).
  Formula: =COUNTIFS(A2:A10, "Present", B2:B10, "HR") -> Result: Present HR Staff count.

[3] AVERAGE, AVERAGEIF, AVERAGEIFS
- Explanation: Un-conditional ya conditional datasets ka average (ausat) nikalna.
- Purpose: Average order value ya category-wise average performance calculate karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =AVERAGEIF(A2:A10, "Delhi", B2:B10) -> Result: Delhi city ki average sale.

[4] SUMPRODUCT
- Explanation: Multiple arrays ke corresponding elements ko pehle multiply karta hai, fir unka total sum nikalta hai.
- Purpose: Bina extra helper column ke (Qty * Rate) ka total bill nikalna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Qty in A2:A4 (2, 3, 5), Rates in B2:B4 (100, 200, 50).
  Formula: =SUMPRODUCT(A2:A4, B2:B4)
  Calculation: (2*100) + (3*200) + (5*50) = 200 + 600 + 250 = 1050

[5] ROUND, ROUNDUP, ROUNDDOWN, INT, TRUNC
- Explanation: Decimals ko round off karna (ROUND = standard, ROUNDUP = higher side, ROUNDDOWN = lower side, INT/TRUNC = decimals hatana).
- Purpose: Financial billing aur tax calculation clear rounded numbers me rakhna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Val = 45.678. Formula: =ROUND(Val, 2) -> Result: 45.68

[6] SUBTOTAL & AGGREGATE
- Explanation: Filtered lists par calculations karte hain aur hidden/filtered-out rows ko ignores kar dete hain.
- Purpose: Dynamic filtered dashboards me accurate totals dikhana.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =SUBTOTAL(9, B2:B100) -> Filtered status ke hisab se visible B column ka sum dega.



Page 3 ke FINANCIAL & INVESTMENT par click karte hi Page 4 par dikhenge:

[1] XIRR & IRR
- Explanation: Investment returns (Internal Rate of Return) calculate karte hain. XIRR non-periodic dates ke cashflows ke liye accurate hai.
- Purpose: Mutual funds, SIPs, aur Business Investments ka annual return rate (CAGR/IRR) nikalna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Dates in A2:A5, Cashflows in B2:B5 (-100000 investment, followed by positive inflows).
  Formula: =XIRR(B2:B5, A2:A5) -> Result: Exact annual return percentage (e.g., 14.5%).

[2] PMT, PPMT, IPMT
- Explanation:
  - PMT: Total monthly EMI calculate karta hai.
  - PPMT: EMI me se Principal (Mool-dhan) amount alag karta hai.
  - IPMT: EMI me se Interest (Byaj) portion alag karta hai.
- Purpose: Bank loans aur Equipment Leasing schedules design karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Loan 5,000,000 (50 Lakhs), Rate 8.5% p.a. (8.5%/12), Tenure 240 Months.
  Formula: =PMT(8.5%/12, 240, -5000000) -> Result: Exact Monthly EMI amount.

[3] NPV & XNPV
- Explanation: Future cash inflows ki Net Present Value calculate karta hai based on a discount rate.
- Purpose: Business project feasibility aur capital budgeting evaluations.
- Scenario & Example:
  Rate 10%, Cashflows B2:B5. Formula: =NPV(10%, B2:B5)

[4] SLN, DB, DDB
- Explanation: Asset Depreciation (SLN = Straight Line, DB/DDB = Declining Balance Method) calculate karte hain.
- Purpose: Company machinery/laptop assets ki yearly book value write-off calculate karna.
- Scenario & Example:
  Cost 100,000, Salvage 10,000, Life 5 Years.
  Formula: =SLN(100000, 10000, 5) -> Result: 18000 per year depreciation.



Page 3 ke DATE & TIME CALCULATIONS par click karte hi Page 4 par dikhenge:

[1] TODAY & NOW
- Explanation: TODAY aaj ki current system date deta hai; NOW date aur current time dono deta hai.
- Purpose: Dynamic aging calculations aur live execution timestamps.
- Scenario & Example:
  Formula: =TODAY() -> Result: 08-09-2026

[2] DATEDIF
- Explanation: Do dates ke beech ka exact difference (Years, Months, ya Days me) nikalta hai.
- Purpose: Age calculation, Employee Tenure, ya Project Duration evaluation.
- Scenario & Example:
  DOB in A2 = 15-08-1995. Current Date = TODAY().
  Formula: =DATEDIF(A2, TODAY(), "Y") -> Result: 31 Years

[3] EDATE & EOMONTH
- Explanation: EDATE N-months aage/peeche ki exact date deta hai. EOMONTH us mahine ki aakhiri date (End of Month) deta hai.
- Purpose: Invoice Due Dates, Subscription Expiry, aur Monthly Financial Closing dates.
- Scenario & Example:
  Invoice Date A2 = 10-01-2026.
  Formula 1 (3 Months Due): =EDATE(A2, 3) -> Result: 10-04-2026
  Formula 2 (Month End): =EOMONTH(A2, 0) -> Result: 31-01-2026

[4] WORKDAY & NETWORKDAYS (INCLUDING .INTL)
- Explanation:
  - NETWORKDAYS: Weekends aur official Holidays ko minus karke working days ginta hai.
  - WORKDAY: Given start date me N working days jod kar target completion date batata hai.
  - .INTL versions custom weekend days (e.g. kewal Sunday off) handle karte hain.
- Purpose: Project SLA tracking aur corporate payroll processing.
- Scenario & Example:
  Start A2 = 01-09-2026, End B2 = 30-09-2026.
  Formula: =NETWORKDAYS.INTL(A2, B2, 11) (Where 11 = Sunday only weekend)
  Result: Total working days in September excluding Sundays.



